WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.660
Dependencies are part of the application.

2
00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:16.120
NuGet packages, transitive dependencies, analyzers, build tools, source generators, runtime versions, container base images, and platform libraries all influence what ships.

3
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:21.420
Introducing or updating a dependency should be reviewed like any other meaningful code change.

4
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.780
Maintenance is continuous.

5
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:30.840
Packages, runtimes, SDKs, operating systems, and deployment platforms need updates over time.

6
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:37.900
Defensive teams make patching an engineering process: monitor, evaluate, test, approve, and deploy.

7
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.800
Waiting until a major incident forces an upgrade usually makes the work harder.

8
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:50.520
Compiler warnings, nullable warnings, analyzers, formatting checks, and static analysis can catch issues earlier.

9
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.340
These tools are not a substitute for design review, but they are useful guardrails.

10
00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:02.320
Treat warnings as feedback about maintainability and correctness, not just noise to suppress.

11
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.360
Build hygiene includes repeatability and credential protection.

12
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:14.860
CI/CD systems may access package feeds, signing keys, deployment credentials, cloud identities, and production environments.

13
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:21.600
Those permissions should be scoped, rotated, audited, and separated by environment where possible.

14
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.600
Security review should be part of normal code review.

15
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:34.140
Reviewers should ask what assumptions changed, what input boundaries were added, what data is now stored or logged, what dependencies changed, and what failure modes were introduced.

16
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.160
Defensive programming scales when it becomes ordinary team practice.
